WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.389
I'm in Gibraltar, a bit of British territory
on the south coast of Europe.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:04.920
Over there is Spain,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.730
and over there, visible on the horizon,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.200
is the north coast of Africa.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.559
In the 1920s,

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:13.680
a German architect named Herman Sörgel saw
an opportunity right here.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.950
He came up with a plan:

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.340
to drain the Mediterranean Sea.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.650
It didn't seem entirely impossible.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.490
The Mediterranean naturally evaporates:

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.330
more water comes in from the Atlantic than
flows out,

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.580
so if you actually managed to put a dam across
the Straits of Gibraltar,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.960
the sea would slowly start to dry up.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.010
Sörgel said that, after the sea level had
dropped maybe 100 or 200 metres,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.980
and a fifth of the water area had turned to
land,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.329
Europe and Africa would be united into one
continent,

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:45.460
and there'd be huge amounts of new land for
settlement and agriculture.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.050
And you could use the dam as a massive hydro
power station,

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.140
on a scale never considered before,

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.930
letting just enough water through to meet
the energy needs of all of his new continent.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.149
Sörgel made his plan public in the years
after the First World War:

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:01.360
perhaps, he thought, all that new land and
exploration

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.069
might be the solution to making sure there
wasn't a Second.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.590
The project was called Atlantropa,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:10.500
and it is one of the most ambitious plans
that has even been announced.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.719
It would have taken an effort on the scale
of the Apollo moon shot.

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:16.520
And it certainly got attention from the press
and the public:

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.359
but there were a few slight problems with
it.

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:25.649
First, there might not have been enough concrete
in the world to build a dam that big,

00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:30.489
let alone all the other secondary dams and
earthworks elsewhere needed to make the project work.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.219
And Sörgel didn't want the dam here,

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:33.619
where the Strait of Gibraltar is narrowest,
no,

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:37.750
he wanted to build it about thirty kilometres
that way, making it much, much longer.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.799
It'd have needed to be 300 metres high,

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:45.329
and the foundations would be two and a half
kilometres wide.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:47.279
But those are practicalities, right?

00:01:47.279 --> 00:01:51.909
Humanity could probably overcome that with
enough effort and enough political will.

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.569
Not that there would have been enough political
will:

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:57.079
the people on the Mediterranean coast would
be, let's say,

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:00.229
rather disappointed that they now lived inland,

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:02.179
perhaps hundreds of miles from the sea.

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:05.600
Venice and Genoa, the folks there, were particularly
unhappy with that.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.940
The plan was popular in his native Germany
but not particularly outside.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.930
And as for Africa: well, it was the early
20th century,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.620
their opinion apparently didn't matter.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.629
It wasn't so much a merging of Europe and
Africa as an attempt at assimilation.

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:21.750
But let's assume that, somehow, over decades,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.829
the Atlantropa project was built.

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:26.590
There would have been some other problems.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.340
All that new land would be salt flats,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.610
completely unusable for growing crops or...
well, for anything, really.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.799
And although Sörgel couldn't have known it
at the time,

00:02:35.799 --> 00:02:40.780
some meteorologists now reckon that the project
might have diverted the Gulf Stream,

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:46.920
sending temperatures plunging in most of Europe
and ending local agriculture as we knew it.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.239
And also, when Sörgel first came up with
his grand plan,

00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:54.390
there likely wasn't a bomb in the world large
enough to destroy a dam that big.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.250
Certainly not in one go, you could just about
make it work...

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.579
but that changed quite quickly during the
Second World War.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:05.400
Having a single point of failure for millions
of people's homes and lives is not ideal.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.849
Sörgel died in 1952, and any hope of the
project died with him,

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:10.620
after he'd spent a lifetime promoting it.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.359
But his idea lives on in science fiction,
and as a testament, perhaps,

00:03:15.359 --> 00:03:17.830
not to how big we can actually build,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:18.469
but just how big we can dream.

